  "stable_key": "g4-lesson-02",
  "position": 2,
  "title": "Determining whether a triangle is right-angled",
  "summary": "Apply the converse of Pythagoras’ theorem to classify triangles from their side lengths and justify the decision.",
  "outcomes": [
    "4.2"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "g4-lesson-01"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Select the longest side as the possible hypotenuse.",
    "Compare the sum of two side squares with the longest-side square.",
    "Use equality or inequality to justify a classification."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-42-converse",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "The converse test",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If three positive lengths are arranged a ≤ b < c, the triangle is right-angled exactly when a² + b² = c². This is the converse of Pythagoras’ theorem. Always identify the longest side before testing."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "a^2+b^2=c^2\\iff\\text{ the triangle is right-angled}"
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g4-lesson-02-converse"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-42-compare",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Interpret the comparison",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Equality proves a right angle opposite c. If a² + b² is greater than c², the largest angle is acute; if it is less than c², the largest angle is obtuse. For this syllabus outcome, show both numerical totals rather than stating a result from appearance."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-42-example-yes",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: confirm a right triangle",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Determine whether side lengths 10 cm, 24 cm and 26 cm form a right triangle."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The possible hypotenuse is 26 cm."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "10^2+24^2=100+576=676"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "26^2=676"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The values are equal, so the triangle is right-angled."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The right angle lies opposite the longest side, 26 cm."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-42-example-no",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: reject a false right triangle",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Determine whether side lengths 8 m, 15 m and 18 m form a right triangle."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "8^2+15^2=289"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "18^2=324"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Because 289 ≠ 324, the triangle is not right-angled."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The calculation uses the longest side 18 m as c; using another side as c would invalidate the test."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-42-validity",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Check that the sides form a triangle",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before classifying, confirm the two shorter sides add to more than the longest side. If a + b ≤ c, the lengths cannot form a non-degenerate triangle. This check matters in unfamiliar or constructed data."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "a+b>c"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-42-errors",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and clear justification",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not compare a + b with c as a right-angle test; that is only the triangle inequality. Do not round square totals. Write the side ordering, both square calculations and a conclusion linked to equality or inequality. Practice questions ask you to enter 1 for right-angled or 0 for not right-angled; work out both totals before you enter it, so that the answer comes from the comparison and not from a guess."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-42-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A triangle has sides 12, 35 and 37. Enter 1 if it is right-angled and 0 otherwise."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "1",
        "canonical_latex": "1",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "12² + 35² = 144 + 1225 = 1369 = 37², so it is right-angled."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-lesson-02-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Order the sides and use the longest as c. Confirm the triangle inequality, then compare a² + b² with c²."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "State the numerical comparison and conclusion; a diagram that looks right-angled is not proof."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
